\chapter{Rope model}

The rope is a chain of forty links, each a small ball, joined neighbour to
neighbour. Each joint keeps the links within one link spacing of each other, and
a joint between neighbours turns off their contact so they never push each
other. Everything else collides. The top link is held by a kinematic grab point
that the player moves. The rope model knows nothing of three.js or React.

The numbers come from the decision gate in the architecture chapter. Solver
iterations, damping and the speed cap are the values that passed it. The cap is
applied after every physics step. It is a deliberate departure from pure
physics, and the gate result records why.

\section{Shapes the rest of the code reads}

The rope model returns a snapshot: the link centres in order, from the grab end
to the free end, and the link radius. Anchors are boxes in the scene. The model
takes them as plain shapes, so it does not depend on what they are for.

@d Rope: snapshot types @{
export interface Vec3 {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  z: number;
}

// The link centres in order, from the grab end to the free end.
export interface RopeSnapshot {
  readonly points: readonly Vec3[];
  readonly radius: number;
}

// An axis-aligned box given by its centre and its half sizes.
export interface Box {
  center: Vec3;
  half: Vec3;
}
@}

\section{Physics model}

The physics engine is loaded once and shared. A rope is created in a world of
its own, so disposing of a rope frees exactly what it made.

The grab point is a kinematic body. A kinematic body is not pushed by contacts,
so the rope never blocks the player's hand. Each link is a dynamic ball with
continuous collision detection on, so a fast drag cannot pass a link through
another. The speed cap is applied to every link after the step, and the step
returns the fastest link speed, which the page uses to decide when the rope has
settled.

@d Rope: physics model @{
import RAPIER from '@@dimforge/rapier3d-compat';
import type { Box, RopeSnapshot, Vec3 } from './snapshot';

export const LINK_COUNT = 40;
export const LINK_RADIUS = 0.02;
export const LINK_SPACING = 0.05;
export const MAX_LINK_SPEED = 3;
const SOLVER_ITERATIONS = 32;
const LINEAR_DAMPING = 0.5;
const GRAVITY = -9.81;

export interface Rope {
  grab(target: Vec3): void;
  step(): number;
  snapshot(): RopeSnapshot;
  dispose(): void;
}

let ready: Promise<void> | null = null;

export function initPhysics(): Promise<void> {
  ready ??= RAPIER.init();
  return ready;
}

export function createRope(top: Vec3, obstacles: readonly Box[]): Rope {
  const world = new RAPIER.World({ x: 0, y: GRAVITY, z: 0 });
  world.numSolverIterations = SOLVER_ITERATIONS;

  const grab = world.createRigidBody(
    RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.kinematicPositionBased().setTranslation(top.x, top.y, top.z),
  );

  const links: RAPIER.RigidBody[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < LINK_COUNT; i++) {
    const body = world.createRigidBody(
      RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()
        .setTranslation(top.x, top.y - i * LINK_SPACING, top.z)
        .setLinearDamping(LINEAR_DAMPING)
        .setCcdEnabled(true),
    );
    world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.ball(LINK_RADIUS), body);
    links.push(body);
  }

  const origin = { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 };
  world.createImpulseJoint(RAPIER.JointData.spherical(origin, origin), grab, links[0], true);
  for (let i = 0; i < LINK_COUNT - 1; i++) {
    const joint = world.createImpulseJoint(
      RAPIER.JointData.rope(LINK_SPACING, origin, origin),
      links[i],
      links[i + 1],
      true,
    );
    joint.setContactsEnabled(false);
  }

  for (const box of obstacles) {
    const body = world.createRigidBody(
      RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.fixed().setTranslation(box.center.x, box.center.y, box.center.z),
    );
    world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(box.half.x, box.half.y, box.half.z), body);
  }

  return {
    grab(target) {
      grab.setNextKinematicTranslation(target);
    },
    step() {
      world.step();
      let fastest = 0;
      for (const body of links) {
        const v = body.linvel();
        const speed = Math.hypot(v.x, v.y, v.z);
        if (speed > MAX_LINK_SPEED) {
          const scale = MAX_LINK_SPEED / speed;
          body.setLinvel({ x: v.x * scale, y: v.y * scale, z: v.z * scale }, true);
        }
        fastest = Math.max(fastest, Math.min(speed, MAX_LINK_SPEED));
      }
      return fastest;
    },
    snapshot() {
      return { points: links.map((body) => body.translation()), radius: LINK_RADIUS };
    },
    dispose() {
      world.free();
    },
  };
}
@}

\section{Tests}

The tests carry the gate criteria forward, so a later change cannot quietly
break the rope. A link may stretch by at most five per cent of the spacing. A
drag must keep every link under the speed cap. A sweep past a bar must not put
any link through the bar.

@d Rope: tests @{
import { beforeAll, describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';
import { LINK_COUNT, LINK_RADIUS, LINK_SPACING, MAX_LINK_SPEED, createRope, initPhysics } from './rope';
import type { Vec3 } from './snapshot';

const TOP: Vec3 = { x: 0, y: 1.5, z: 0 };

// The largest neighbour distance, divided by the spacing. One means taut.
function longestStretch(points: readonly Vec3[]): number {
  let longest = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < points.length - 1; i++) {
    const a = points[i];
    const b = points[i + 1];
    longest = Math.max(longest, Math.hypot(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y, a.z - b.z) / LINK_SPACING);
  }
  return longest;
}

beforeAll(async () => {
  await initPhysics();
});

describe('rope model', () => {
  it('hangs from the grab with no link stretched beyond five per cent', () => {
    const rope = createRope(TOP, []);
    for (let s = 0; s < 300; s++) rope.step();
    const { points } = rope.snapshot();
    expect(points).toHaveLength(LINK_COUNT);
    expect(longestStretch(points)).toBeLessThan(1.05);
    rope.dispose();
  });

  it('keeps every link under the speed cap while dragged in a circle', () => {
    const rope = createRope(TOP, []);
    for (let s = 0; s < 300; s++) rope.step();
    const radius = 0.4;
    const omega = 1.5 / radius;
    let fastest = 0;
    for (let s = 0; s < 360; s++) {
      const t = s / 60;
      rope.grab({ x: radius * Math.sin(omega * t), y: TOP.y, z: radius * (1 - Math.cos(omega * t)) });
      fastest = Math.max(fastest, rope.step());
    }
    expect(fastest).toBeLessThanOrEqual(MAX_LINK_SPEED);
    expect(longestStretch(rope.snapshot().points)).toBeLessThan(1.05);
    rope.dispose();
  });

  it('does not pass a link through a bar it is swept across', () => {
    const bar = { center: { x: 0.3, y: 0, z: 0 }, half: { x: 0.01, y: 1, z: 0.01 } };
    const rope = createRope({ x: -0.1, y: 1.5, z: 0 }, [bar]);
    for (let s = 0; s < 120; s++) rope.step();
    let inside = 0;
    for (let s = 0; s < 120; s++) {
      rope.grab({ x: -0.1 + 0.9 * (s / 120), y: 1.5, z: 0 });
      rope.step();
      for (const p of rope.snapshot().points) {
        // A link centre inside the bar, grown by half a radius, is a pass-through.
        const margin = 0.01 + 0.5 * LINK_RADIUS;
        if (Math.abs(p.x - bar.center.x) < margin && Math.abs(p.z) < margin && Math.abs(p.y) < 1) inside++;
      }
    }
    expect(inside).toBe(0);
    rope.dispose();
  });
});
@}
